<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<!--
	  ZenPom's privacy manifest.

	  APPLE DOES NOT REQUIRE THIS FILE, AND IT IS HERE ANYWAY.
	  A manifest is required when an app uses a required-reason API or collects
	  data. Nothing is collected, and exactly one required-reason API is used:
	  `UserDefaults`, in exactly one file, holding one sprint's shape. Declared
	  below with its reason code, and `PolishFenceTests` holds the "one file"
	  half of that claim to the tree.

	  THIS FILE CHANGED UNDER `D32`, AND IT IS A SECOND ARTEFACT OF THAT DELTA.
	  The delta was written about a test fence and this manifest was not costed
	  in it. It is recorded here rather than smoothed over: a feature that emits
	  a second artefact nothing checks is the failure `conventions.md` names
	  under "validate every artefact, not just the primary one".

	  So this exists to make a claim CHECKABLE rather than to satisfy a rule.
	  `SPEC.md` says local only, no analytics. That is prose, and prose is what
	  this project has repeatedly found to be true in a document and false in the
	  code. This is the same sentence in a form App Store Connect reads and a test
	  can hold.

	  Every array below is empty on purpose. An empty array is a statement; a
	  missing key is a silence.
	-->

	<!-- No tracking, and no domains to name. -->
	<key>NSPrivacyTracking</key>
	<false/>
	<key>NSPrivacyTrackingDomains</key>
	<array/>

	<!--
	  NOTHING IS COLLECTED. The distraction log, the timer's history and the
	  Todoist mirror all live in SwiftData on the device and are read by nobody
	  else. The export leaves the phone only when a person shares it, by their own
	  action, to a destination they pick.

	  Todoist and MusicKit are network calls, and neither is collection: one reads
	  a task list the person already owns and ticks one item off, the other plays
	  their own library. No data about the person is sent anywhere by this app.
	-->
	<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypes</key>
	<array/>

	<!--
	  ONE required-reason API, and it is `UserDefaults`. CA92.1 is Apple's reason
	  code for reading and writing your own app's defaults, for your own app's
	  use — which is the whole of what happens here: one encoded value holding
	  the shape of the sprint you are in, read by this app and by nothing else,
	  deleted when the sprint ends. Nothing about a person is read from it or
	  written to it, and it never leaves the device.

	  If a second one is ever added — file timestamps, disk space, system boot
	  time, active keyboard — it belongs here with its reason code, and
	  `PrivacyManifestTests` fails until it is.
	-->
	<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypes</key>
	<array>
		<dict>
			<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPIType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyAccessedAPICategoryUserDefaults</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypeReasons</key>
			<array>
				<string>CA92.1</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
	</array>
</dict>
</plist>
